column3:
@kannes 

The intention is not that this becomes the "written in stone, forever" policy. It's rather a "lets start with a closed door, which we can then selectively open a little if/when it's safe and proper discussion has been made on that". 

So we start by being ultra-restrictive, and then, later, someone (else) argues for cases like "ai can be used as a replacement for ide autocomplete" / "ai can be used for language translations" / etc , all to be considered on a singular case-by-case basis.

@uclaros 

It's intended as a correction to the earlier QEP. That clearly didn't work (as you've pointed out, the process didn't happen in reality, and we've already lost at least one extremely high value contributor as a result).

So as noted above, we correct by slamming the door shut, and then choosing later how far we want to inch it open.

> I think the measure of compliance is "did you in good conscience write the code yourself" - we are not set up to peer into your computer and look at what you did. You will know if your brain made the code you want to share or someone else's brain did.,

@timlinux Yes, but there you also included the verb "write" which is missing from the current proposal. It might certainly be implied, but explicit is better than implicit (to cite the Zen of Python).

Edit to clarify: I don't mind the intention of the policy (especially when it's just about writing), it's just the wording I'm worried about.
